<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Washukanni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Washukanni was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>, probably located in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> valley of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, though its exact site has not been confirmed. It was sacked by the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> around 1340 BCE. This was a decisive blow to Mitanni's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/washukanni.jpg" alt="Washukanni" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N-Mesopotamia_and_Syria_english.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Waššukanni, also written Aššukanni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian</a> texts, was the capital of the Hurrian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> from around 1500 BCE to the 13th century BCE. Its name may be of Old Indo-Aryan origin, from an original *Vasukanni built on the Indic word vasu, good, with an Indic suffix -ka- and the Hurrian suffix -nni.</p><p>Its site is unknown. Most proposals lie between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaghjagh-river">Jaghjagh River</a> in the upper Jezirah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Dietrich Opitz placed it under the largely unexcavated mound of Tell el Fakhariya near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Tell Halaf</a>; a neutron activation comparison with clay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> tablets seemed to rule this out, and Edward Lipinski rejected it, but recent German excavations led Stefano de Martino, Mirko Novák and Dominik Bonatz to support it again. No text naming the capital has yet been found there. Tell Farfara, Üçtepe Höyük in Diyarbakır Province, Koçlu Tepe and Tell al-Hawa have also been proposed, and the route of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a>'s campaign suggests a place near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a>, who reigned c. 1344–1322 BCE, sacked the city early in his reign. In his own account he passed through Alse, captured Kutmar, ransacked Suta and reached Waššukanni, while Tusratta, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, withdrew and would not meet him in battle; the Hittite king then recrossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halba">Halba</a> and Mukis. His treaty inscription records that he installed a Hurrian vassal king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shattiwaza">Shattiwaza</a>. Around 1290 BCE the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-i">Adad-nirari I</a> sacked the city again, and it served for a time as an Assyrian provincial capital before disappearing from history. Its name survives in the Waşokanî refugee camp near Hesekê in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, built for people of Serê Kaniyê and Zirgan displaced by the Turkish offensive of 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Waššukanni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cities such as the capital <b>Washukanni</b>, whose site has not been securely identified, were political and economic centres that housed artisans, merchants and administrative offices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
